UID:064e02abb7fe444d@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260806T143300Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Air Plan Approval\; Illinois\; 2015 Ozone Mod
 erate and Serious Reasonably Available Control Technology Update
DESCRIPTION:The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is proposing to
  approve revisions to 35 Illinois Administrative Code (IAC) parts 217\, 21
 8\, and 219 into the Illinois State Implementation Plan (SIP). The Illinoi
 s Environmental Protection Agency (Illinois or Illinois EPA) submitted the
 se revisions on December 18\, 2024\, and May 12\, 2025\, supplemented thei
 r submittal on August 13\, 2025\, for Moderate Reasonably Available Contro
 l Technology (RACT). The EPA is proposing to approve 35 IAC parts 217\, 21
 8\, and 219 as satisfying the Moderate Volatile Organic Compound (VOC) RAC
 T and NO\nX\nRACT requirements as well as Serious NO\nX\nRACT requirements
  for the Chicago\, IL (Cook County\, DuPage County\, Grundy County\, Kane 
 County\, Kendall County\, Lake County\, McHenry County\, and Will County) 
 and Metro-East St. Louis (Madison County\, Monroe County\, and St. Clair C
 ounty) nonattainment areas under the 2015 ozone National Ambient Air Quali
 ty Standard (NAAQS or standard).\nhttps://www.federalregister.gov/document
 s/2026/08/06/2026-16001/air-plan-approval-illinois-2015-ozone-moderate-and
 -serious-reasonably-available-control-technology
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/06/2026-16001/air-pl
 an-approval-illinois-2015-ozone-moderate-and-serious-reasonably-available-
 control-technology
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c2bdd98e2e202891@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260806T143250Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as Particl
 e Pollution Levels Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges
DESCRIPTION:Maine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as Particle Pollution Level
 s Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges\nMaine Department of Environme
 ntal Protection sent this bulletin at 07/17/2026 03:23 PM EDT\n--- PRESS R
 ELEASE ---\nFor Immediate Release\nMaine DEP Issues Air Quality Alert as P
 article Pollution Levels Predicted to Escalate to Unhealthy Ranges\nAUGUST
 A\, July 17\, 2026\n— The Maine Department of Environmental Protection w
 arns that ground-level particle pollution concentrations are forecasted to
  reach the ‘Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups’ (USG) level this Saturday 
 due to wildfire smoke drifting in from Manitoba and Ontario\, Canada.\nThe
  smoke plume is expected to move back over Maine during the day on Saturda
 y\, with air quality measured by a 24-hour average forecasted at the USG l
 evel. Some hours may experience particle pollution levels even higher than
  this range. Residents are encouraged to follow health recommendations tha
 t suit their individual needs.\nSmoke conditions may improve by midday Sun
 day\, but due to the unpredictable nature of wildfire smoke\, the Maine De
 partment of Environmental Protection advises checking their website or cal
 ling the toll-free Air Quality Hotline at 800-223-1196 for the latest upda
 tes throughout the weekend.\nElevated particle pollution levels can affect
  sensitive groups such as children\, older adults\, outdoor workers\, and 
 individuals with respiratory or heart conditions including asthma\, bronch
 itis\, and COPD. Symptoms may include coughing\, shortness of breath\, thr
 oat irritation\, and mild chest pain. Even healthy adults engaging in outd
 oor physical activity might notice these effects.\nSome actions you can ta
 ke to protect your health during such periods include:\nAvoiding strenuous
  outdoor activity\nClosing windows and circulating indoor air with a fan o
 r air conditioner\nAsthmatics should keep their quick-relief medications a
 nd action plan handy\nAdditional health information may be found on the fo
 llowing websites:\nMaine CDC asthma prevention an\nhttps://content.govdeli
 very.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/420fa43
URL:https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/420fa43
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9466cceffccea1bf@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260806T143250Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] August 2026
DESCRIPTION:O&M Newsletter\, August 2026\nMaine Department of Environmenta
 l Protection sent this bulletin at 08/05/2026 04:12 PM EDT\nAUGUST 2026\nI
 n this issue:\nBreaking: Active Cyber Breaches Affecting Public Water Syst
 ems Demand Immediate Action.\nCISA Alert to Water and Wastewater Systems R
 egarding Cybersecurity Threats.\nStay Informed: Key Updates on Rulemaking 
 for Chapters 595 and 579!\nState Laboratory Certification Required for In-
 House Testing of Nitrogen and Phosphorus Samples.\nEPA Announces WIFIA App
 lication Fee Waiver for Small Communities.\nDMR-QA 2026 Update.\nTraining 
 Highlights.\nFor Practice.\nBreaking: Active Cyber Breaches Affecting Publ
 ic Water Systems Demand Immediate Action.\nThe US EPA\, FBI\, Cybersecurit
 y & Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA)\, and Water Information Sharing 
 & Analysis Center (WaterISAC) have held urgent briefings regarding\nactive
  and on-going cybersecurity breaches at dozens of Public Water Systems (PW
 Ss) in at least seven states across the U.S.\nThe targeted PWSs range in s
 ize from small to large.\nMany wastewater systems use similar technology a
 s found in the drinking water sector. A common symptom of these breaches i
 ncludes a loss of PLC connectivity with devices. These incidents all invol
 ve vulnerabilities related to PLCs\, as described in the\nIranian-Affiliat
 ed Cyber Actors Exploit Programmable Logic Controllers Across US Critical 
 Infrastructure\nadvisory (PDF).\nThe Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI)
  has information and guidance regarding malicious cyber actors targeting P
 LCs.\nMalicious Cyber Actors Targeting Water Sector Internet-Facing PLCs.p
 df\nRestoring Access to a MicroLogix 1400 Controller - Password Unknown.pd
 f\n"Malicious Cyber Actors Targeting Water and Wastewater Sector Internet-
 Facing Programmable Logic Controllers\, Causing Operational Disruptions" i
 s a brief description of the current threat landscape pertaining to PLCs.
 \n"Restoring Access to a MicroLogix\nTM\n1400 Controller When the Password
  is Unknown" offers guidance on restoring acce\nhttps://content.govdeliver
 y.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/421a6b4
URL:https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/MEDEP/bulletins/421a6b4
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f484726943ac75c5@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260804T195436Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Penquis C.A.P.\, Inc. v. Department of Admini
 strative and Financial Services et al.
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
   2026 ME 79 \nDocket: BCD-25-275  \nArgued: February 4\, 2026   \nDecided
 :  August 4\, 2026 \n \nPanel:  STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, CONNORS\, LAW
 RENCE\, AND DOUGLAS\, JJ.  \n \n \nPENQUIS C.A.P.\, INC. \n \nv. \n \nDEPA
 RTMENT OF ADMINISTRATIVE AND FINANCIAL SERVICES et al. \n \n \nSTANFILL\, 
 C.J. \n[¶1]  The Maine Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) \nc
 ontracts with private entities to provide medical nonemergency \ntransport
 ation (NET) services for individuals eligible for MaineCare or the \nChild
 ren’s Health Insurance Program.  In 2023\, DHHS conducted a competitive 
 \nbidding process to award a new NET service contract for each of Maine’
 s eight \ntransit regions\, see 5 M.R.S. §§ 1825-B to 1825-D (2026)\;\n1
  18-554 C.M.R. ch. 110 \n(effective Apr. 22\, 2010) \, and it ultimately a
 warded contracts for all eight \nregions to ModivCare Solutions\, LLC  (Mo
 divCare).  Aggrieved bidders \nchallenged the awards for several of the re
 gions by  bringing administrative \n \n1  Several provisions of the procur
 ement statute have been amended since 2023\, but none of those \namendment
 s are relevant to this appeal.  We thus cite the most recent version of th
 e Maine Revised \nStatutes in our references to the procurement statute. \
 n 2 \nappeals in the Department of Administrative and Financial Services (
 DAFS)\, see \n5 M.R.S. § 1825-E (2026)\; 18-554 C.M.R. ch. 120 (effective
  May 24\, 1995) \, and \nthen seeking judicial review in the Superior Cour
 t \, see 5 M.R.S. §§ 1825- F\, \n11001(1) (2026)\; M.R. Civ. P. 80C.  We
  recently rejected a challenge by Waldo \nCommunity Action Partners (Waldo
  CAP) to the award for Region 5\, see Waldo \nCmty. Action Partners v. Dep
 ’t of Admin. & Fin. Servs. ( Waldo CAP)\, 2026 ME 13\, \n¶¶ 18\, 40\, 
 354 A.3d 319\, and we now address a challenge by Penquis C.A.P.\, Inc. \n(
 Penquis CAP) \, to the awards for Regions 2\, 3\, 4\, and 8.  For the reas
 ons \nexplained below\, we affirm the judgment of the Business and Consume
 r Docket \n(McKeon\, J.) upholdi\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/
 lawcourt/2026/26me079.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me079.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a6165478c3ce6280@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260804T143749Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Man
 agement Act Provisions\; Fisheries of the Northeastern United States\; Nor
 theast Multispecies Fishery\; Framework Adjustment 72
DESCRIPTION:NMFS issues this interim final rule to implement Framework Adj
 ustment 72 (Framework 72) to the Northeast Multispecies Fishery Management
  Plan (FMP)\, specifically to implement status determination criteria for 
 Georges Bank yellowtail flounder\, set catch limits for multispecies (grou
 ndfish) stocks\, and revise the process for setting recreational measures 
 for Atlantic cod and haddock. This action is necessary to respond to updat
 ed scientific information and to achieve the goals and objectives of the F
 MP. The measures are intended to help prevent overfishing\, rebuild overfi
 shed stocks\, achieve optimum yield\, and ensure that management measures 
 are based on the best scientific information available.\nhttps://www.feder
 alregister.gov/documents/2026/08/04/2026-15784/magnuson-stevens-fishery-co
 nservation-and-management-act-provisions-fisheries-of-the-northeastern
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/04/2026-15784/magnus
 on-stevens-fishery-conservation-and-management-act-provisions-fisheries-of
 -the-northeastern
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
